data/generation/deep/singing.json:
{
  "about": "The Stone-Singers' hall under a hill: a deep site generated from what it was for. They shaped stone by song, cutting their halls whole out of the living rock without a mark of chisel or a joint, and their doors opened to a note; at the last, failing, they sang the last of themselves into the stone of their hall. Levels follow the singing down: the porch and its hall of doors, the choir where they sang, and the still halls where the song stopped, down a long hall to where it was still cutting the rock when it failed. A pivot-door that has settled on its stone closes the stair; the door of the still halls opens only to the note cut over it, for whoever can read it; a crack the unsung stone opened is a way round, for a climber or a rope; a sounding-shaft where the wind sings is a second way out. What the singers sang into the stone keeps the last hall, and their own knell stills it, rung on the cantors' bell: not a priest's prayers, but their bell, which the last of the cantors took down and lies with in the unfinished hall. Written by hand.",
